"explanation": "This question asks to evaluate the strength of evidence for the endosymbiotic theory. The presence of their own circular DNA (A), prokaryote-like ribosomes (B), and their similar size to bacteria (C) are all strong pieces of evidence that support their origin as independent prokaryotic cells. They have retained remnants of their ancestral prokaryotic machinery. Option D, however, is factually incorrect and provides no support. Mitochondria and chloroplasts are located in the cytoplasm of eukaryotic cells, *outside* the nucleus. The theory is about them becoming organelles *within* the cytoplasm of a host cell, not within its nucleus. This makes the statement not only wrong but also completely irrelevant as a piece of supporting evidence, thus being the weakest (and incorrect) point."
