"explanation": "Reproductive barriers are mechanisms that prevent different species from interbreeding. They are categorized as prezygotic (acting before the formation of a zygote) or postzygotic (acting after the zygote is formed). Mating at different times (A, temporal isolation), having different courtship rituals (B, behavioral isolation), and gametic incompatibility (D, gametic isolation) are all prezygotic barriers because they prevent fertilization from ever occurring. A postzygotic barrier is one that operates after a hybrid zygote has been formed. The production of a mule from a horse-donkey cross is a classic example. A zygote is formed, and an offspring develops, but that hybrid offspring (the mule) is sterile and cannot reproduce. This is called 'reduced hybrid fertility' and is a postzygotic barrier that keeps the horse and donkey gene pools separate."
